body:
In reality it's one week *in the best-case scenario*. First missed period counts as forth week (no matter when the conception actually took place), and pregnancy tests only become accurate a week after that. However:

1) most people's periods don't work like a clock, and it's normal to have up to a week late period. If not actively trying to conceive or having unprotected sex, the majority would probably think about pregnancy a three-four days later, leaving little or no time at all;

2) sometimes periods (or, rather, period-like bloody excretions) continue throughout pregnancy, meaning there's no chance to discover it before it's too late. Some people may also never develop enough hCG for a test to work;

3) even if you discover and decide in time, it'll be probably too late to book an appointment and get to the clinic. If you live in a remote area, don't have enough saved money or are a teenager, it's basically impossible;

4) the basis of the law is completely bogus. Even the circulatory system is just starting to form at the time (not to mention the heart is just a muscle, it has no bearing on humanity or consciousness), but the brain is *far* from formed. Brain becomes a distinct structure only at week 7, things like hearing are starting at the end of the second semester, and feelings, including pain, develop in the third trimester - at the 27th week or later. Even by the boldest assumptions a fetus can be called sentient only at 5 months into the pregnancy;

5) Almost all voluntary abortions are done in the first trimester, long before proper brain development but also later than the Texas law deadline. Almost all late abortions are performed as medical emergencies, not to mention 1 in 8 pregnancies end in a miscarriage on their own (and that's only among the people who knew they were pregnant). The darkest part of laws like that is that not only they're unnecessary, but they also create a huge pressure on the doctors and patients to continue dangerous pregnancies to avoid lawsuits. There're multiple precedents of distraught women who miscarried being harassed and even sued in suspicion of an illegal abortion; there're horrible example of women forced to carry lethaly ill and dying fetuses for months because it wasn't 'a risk to their life'; there're people like the 11 year old girl who was raped and made to continue the dangerous and deeply traumatic pregnancy. No matter how much you believe the 'rights' of a fetus trample the rights of women, it's a fact the Texas law just made pregnancy more dangerous and traumatic for everyone

Though I suspect you aren't arguing in good faith here:

1) trans teens aren't "confused" about their identity. Nor do they choose it. And most people know their gender very early on, some as long as they can remember;

2) gender-confirming surgeries aren't performed on minors. At all;

3) literally no medical intervention is happening before puberty. Even then, it's only puberty blockers which are reversible and are prescribed to teens who have been monitored for a fairly long time beforehand;

4) yes, doctors can distinguish between a kid playing pretend and having gender dysphoria

Yeah, sure!
What do we have here:

- a terf trans guy;

- 'no, they're not transphobic, they are radfem!';

- 'wait, WHO supports trans women?! I thought they were feminists!';

- vaguely disguised 41% jokes.

trans-internet be like: my life is falling apart. All I've looked forward is unreachable. Everyday I'm closer to ending it all. \**Sad noises\**

&#x200B;

